:answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5402e2e-2a63-5f18-9868-604f33f81924" :question/math-academy-id "q-29360" :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,\\frac{5}{4}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110733 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6fa9e7b-172d-57b6-8b6e-0db0f8f802ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements about Exponential Growth Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e229a88-7e42-58dd-a8d6-ae3390023bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507656} {:answer/id #uuid "46a450a1-a396-59e1-9a6b-cb0e767f8fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507708} {:answer/id #uuid "70bfc9bf-2885-5b6b-92ef-d910c905221b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507761} {:answer/id #uuid "961b0c01-83be-5cb1-9a7b-ff49a971b18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507810} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5675ff-f62b-5eb3-9643-02cc5d50e980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a450a1-a396-59e1-9a6b-cb0e767f8fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507708} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e7eb3d-b4ba-5f6a-8826-1bca21045615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c063995-cffa-55cf-8462-64cb05fc0c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-46059" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to +\\,\\infty $ as $x\\to +\\,\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/45/4531a531fb98f092751cec4552d6ead2bf828759842214f030618dba1ea85893.png)\n\n \n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x>0,$ the graph increases without a bound as $x$ increases to infinity.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110733 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6fa9e7b-172d-57b6-8b6e-0db0f8f802ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements about Exponential Growth Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "622a920b-f7cc-50a7-a623-080277e44780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507742} {:answer/id #uuid "6a08bbff-2d42-5a8c-b055-690e677fc271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507753} {:answer/id #uuid "db1a3679-96b2-5175-9edf-4e5f2b677211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507893} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb7e80c-90dc-5734-81f1-382ec59c5e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507919} {:answer/id #uuid "f0703391-9746-55e8-bdc7-282159419398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0703391-9746-55e8-bdc7-282159419398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507924} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c0019f1-586d-574b-93ab-bd726d43763d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "302012e2-b0bf-56ed-8145-9c936fb36ce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-47739" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={5}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $(-\\infty ,5).$\n\nII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to -\\,\\infty .$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={5}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/31/31c990964a5a04c4a44773bb00d1b097999951b823e3b10861d13e38c364b8b5.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of $f(x)={5}^{x}$ is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true, since for $x<0$, the graph gets close to zero as we decrease $x.$\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7654997-ee9a-5d6c-88e9-d46f36bd8e88" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Stretched Exponential Growth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb64125-a7d3-5b49-a2f6-4235214e697f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507619} {:answer/id #uuid "2e918a36-d84a-52b6-8a6f-fe07207b7c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507658} {:answer/id #uuid "4602128f-5d65-569b-8d8a-ca3d5b50a532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507705} {:answer/id #uuid "98d88c1c-d97c-59d0-b056-2b17db6467ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507811} {:answer/id #uuid "e3de98f4-1e5b-5aeb-821f-b3abb97bccf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e918a36-d84a-52b6-8a6f-fe07207b7c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507658} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f74135-1719-59e7-a4de-fc90530e85bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d727472-a16c-5704-a3dd-e6b93f9d685f" :question/math-academy-id "q-7118" :question/problem "![](images/af/afeee9a0b7dd8a68368444ca475a6ed9493f0dfb007f4e8406b8fe2a32abf9e4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({2}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k({2}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={2}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,2)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110734 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7654997-ee9a-5d6c-88e9-d46f36bd8e88" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Stretched Exponential Growth Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd2e8e0-9905-54e6-81a0-d7a3b82ee823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507661} {:answer/id #uuid "357d6e13-353c-5021-823b-660196a0ea45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507674} {:answer/id #uuid "55f3c50c-4ff4-58ee-962e-908f5dbf77e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507726} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb8905f-4868-5bfb-a51f-7a6bef705efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507785} {:answer/id #uuid "dce2766b-d402-5cd1-b80d-96bf1f25fb39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb8905f-4868-5bfb-a51f-7a6bef705efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507785} :answer-field/id #uuid "3338c64b-fdc6-5d7e-9864-167da0a1603e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f281af5-b28c-5775-bf2f-eca0f2d16ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-7235" :question/problem "![](images/90/90626ef73fbd9c0bd94802275961ca0c604a76f0d01faa1889b7dd3524ad6132.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k({4}^{x}).$ What is the value of the constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k({4}^{x})$ we take the graph of $y={4}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$\n\nComparing the point $(0,3)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=3.$"}]}
